        "DEFINITION": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "canonical_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Toxicosis due to ingestion of sleep aids or antianxiety medications commonly used in human and veterinary medicine.",
              "pdf_page": 251,
              "printed_page": 169,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Benzodiazepine class—alprazolam (Xanax®), clonazepam (Klonopin®), diazepam (Valium®), lorazepam (Ativan®), midazolam (Versed®), temazepam (Restoril®), triazolam (Halcion®), and many more.",
              "pdf_page": 251,
              "printed_page": 169,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Imidazopyridine class—eszopiclone (Lunesta®), zaleplon (Sonata®), and zolpidem (Ambien®, Ambien CR, Intermezzo®, Zolpimist®).",
              "pdf_page": 251,
              "printed_page": 169,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "canonical_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Benzodiazepines and imidazopyridines bind to receptors near the gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA) receptor/chloride channel on neurons; they potentiate GABA’s effect, which increases the opening of the chloride channel, leading to hyperpolarization of the nerve and decreased excitation.",
              "pdf_page": 251,
              "printed_page": 169,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Imidazopyridines bind near the receptor subset that is responsible for sedation, while benzodiazepines bind to all receptor subsets and so not only mediate sedation, but are also anticonvulsant and anxiolytic.",
              "pdf_page": 251,
              "printed_page": 169,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Paradoxical reactions can occur and are typically described as excitement, irritability, and aberrant demeanor in cats and excitement in dogs, when the expected effect is seizure control or sedation.",
              "pdf_page": 251,
              "printed_page": 169,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Both classes are well absorbed orally and have rapid onset of actions, often less than 30 minutes.",
              "pdf_page": 251,
              "printed_page": 169,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Duration of action depends on the drug and may last for hours to days.",
              "pdf_page": 251,
              "printed_page": 169,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Both classes have wide margins of safety; lethal exposures are rare if a single agent is involved.",
              "pdf_page": 251,
              "printed_page": 169,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Benzodiazepines—signs can be seen at therapeutic doses; however, the drugs have a wide margin of safety, with the minimal lethal dose being approximately 1,000 times the therapeutic dose. Cats may develop idiosyn cratic hepatic failure with chronic oral dosing of diazepam and clonazepam.",
              "pdf_page": 251,
              "printed_page": 169,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Zaleplon—based on a review of the American Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals’ Animal Poison Control Center (ASPCA APCC) Antox database: in",
              "pdf_page": 251,
              "printed_page": 169,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "subheading",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "dogs, doses>0.11 mg/kg have been associated",
              "pdf_page": 251,
              "printed_page": 169,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "with restlessness and hyperactivity; in cats,",
              "pdf_page": 251,
              "printed_page": 169,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "dogs, doses>0.11 mg/kg have been associated"
            },
            {
              "type": "subheading",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "doses of>1.25 mg/kg caused paradoxical",
              "pdf_page": 251,
              "printed_page": 169,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "reactions.",
              "pdf_page": 251,
              "printed_page": 169,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "doses of>1.25 mg/kg caused paradoxical"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Zolpidem—based on a review of the",
              "pdf_page": 251,
              "printed_page": 169,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "doses of>1.25 mg/kg caused paradoxical"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "ASPCA APCC Antox database: in dogs,",
              "pdf_page": 251,
              "printed_page": 169,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "doses of>1.25 mg/kg caused paradoxical"
            },
            {
              "type": "subheading",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "dosages>0.2 mg/kg can cause mild sedation",
              "pdf_page": 251,
              "printed_page": 169,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "subheading",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "and ataxia; doses>0.6 mg/kg can cause",
              "pdf_page": 251,
              "printed_page": 169,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "paradoxical reactions. In cats, signs of",
              "pdf_page": 251,
              "printed_page": 169,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "and ataxia; doses>0.6 mg/kg can cause"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "paradoxical reactions were seen at 0.34 mg/kg",
              "pdf_page": 251,
              "printed_page": 169,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "and ataxia; doses>0.6 mg/kg can cause"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "or greater.",
              "pdf_page": 251,
              "printed_page": 169,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "and ataxia; doses>0.6 mg/kg can cause"
            }
          ]
        },
